+
+## Definition
+
+Use natural units $c=\hbar=1$. Let $M$ be a compact oriented smooth four-manifold with a Riemannian metric and with the spin, gauge, matter, auxiliary, and R-symmetry bundles supplied as part of a rigid-background package. For each declared field species $a$, let $E_a\to M$ be its specified vector bundle. Put $E^{\bar 0}=\bigoplus_{a\ {\rm bosonic}}E_a$ for bosonic and auxiliary fields and $E^{\bar 1}=\bigoplus_{a\ {\rm fermionic\ or\ ghost}}E_a$ for fermion and ghost fields. The grading is parity modulo two. The space of smooth field configurations is a specified subset of $\Gamma^\infty(M,E^{\bar 0}\oplus E^{\bar 1})$ satisfying the declared reality, contour, gauge, and boundary conditions; the smooth sections are the common classical domain unless a different domain is stated.
+
+A gauge-fixed supersymmetry fluctuation datum at a smooth $Q$-fixed configuration $\Phi_*$ consists of the field bundles, the declared gauge-fixing condition, its Faddeev–Popov operator, the ghost and auxiliary fields, and the odd gauge-fixed transformation $Q$ on every field. Here $Q$ is the sum of the selected off-shell supersymmetry transformation and the specified gauge-fixing or BRST differential. Its square $H=Q^2$ is part of the data and must be given on every field; it may be a nonzero combination of gauge, spacetime, R-symmetry, and flavor transformations. For the round-sphere model used in this page, the total gauge-fixed convention is $Q=s+\delta$ and $H=\delta_{\rm gauge}(a_0)+\delta_{U(1)}(\omega)$ on fields and ghosts, with $\delta_{\rm gauge}(u)A_\mu=-D_\mu u$ on the connection and $\delta_{\rm gauge}(u)X=[u,X]$ on adjoint tensors. Thus $Q$ is not called nilpotent unless $H$ acts as zero on the specified sector.
+
+The linearized fluctuation operator is the derivative $\mathcal D_{\Phi_*}=(dQ)_{\Phi_*}$ acting on tangent fluctuations, which are sections of $E^{\bar 0}\oplus E^{\bar 1}$ obeying the linearized gauge and boundary conditions. If $Q$ is a finite-order differential transformation, $\mathcal D_{\Phi_*}$ is a matrix of finite-order differential operators. The relation $Q^2=H$ implies $\mathcal D_{\Phi_*}^2=(dH)_{\Phi_*}$ at a $Q$-fixed configuration; it is a cochain differential on the subspace on which this linearized symmetry acts as zero. For operator domains on a compact manifold, one may choose Sobolev completions $W^{k,2}(M,E_a)$ defined by requiring all weak covariant derivatives through order $k$ to be square-integrable with respect to the chosen metric and bundle metric. An operator of order $m$ then has the stated domain $W^{k,2}$ and codomain $W^{k-m,2}$ for a declared integer $k\ge m$; the bundle metrics, connections, boundary conditions, and closed-operator realization are additional data.
+
+For a differential operator $D$ of order $m$, its principal symbol $\sigma_m(D)(x,\xi)$ is the homogeneous degree-$m$ part obtained in local coordinates by replacing each highest-order derivative $\partial_\mu$ by $i\xi_\mu$. Any claim of ellipticity or transverse ellipticity must identify the operator, the relevant symbol sequence, and the symmetry directions removed from that sequence; this definition asserts none of those properties. A zero mode of $D$ is a vector in its specified domain that lies in $\ker D$. A Faddeev–Popov zero mode is a vector in the kernel of the specified Faddeev–Popov operator, and an infinitesimal gauge stabilizer is a gauge transformation that fixes the background connection. A one-loop determinant is a regularized determinant or ratio of determinants of specified fluctuation operators with their domains, spectra, phases, and zero-mode conventions fixed. A primed determinant omits only the stated kernel; the omitted modes require separate treatment. No determinant follows from the symbol or from a formal supercharge algebra alone.
+
+A finite-dimensional regulator is extra data, not a consequence of the continuum fluctuation datum. It consists of a finite-dimensional oriented smooth field-parameter manifold $\mathfrak F_\rho$, a smooth positive volume form, an integration cycle and boundary conditions, a graded algebra identified with $\Omega^\bullet(\mathfrak F_\rho;\mathbb C)$, a graded integral of its top-degree part, and an odd derivation $Q_\rho$ induced by the declared field transformation. Its square is the induced symmetry $H_\rho$. In this page the finite-dimensional integration and deformation data use the exact conventions of the finite-dimensional Q-exact localization definition: $Q_\rho$-invariance means the integral of $Q_\rho F$ vanishes on the admissible integrands, including the zero-boundary-flux condition. This data does not identify the formal noncommutative S4 regulator with a finite-dimensional regulator, and it does not construct an infinite-dimensional field-space measure.
+
+## Definition references
+
+[[def-qftx-susy-background-data|Rigid background and gauge-fixing data]]
+
+[[def-finite-dimensional-q-exact-localization-data|Finite-dimensional Q-exact integration data]]
+
+[Schwarz and Zaboronsky, Sections 2–3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/9511112)
+
+[Pestun and Zabzine, Section 1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1608.02953)
+
+[Pestun, Sections 2–4](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0712.2824)
+
+[Peelaers, Sections 1.1–1.2](https://indico.math.cnrs.fr/event/3026/attachments/2163/2457/Peelaers-LectureNotes.pdf)
